---
// WHAT WORKS. One row per thing a Salesforce developer has a name for.
//
// This page used to publish the 14 internal capability FAMILIES flat, which are our tier
// boundaries and not anyone else's vocabulary: `schema` appeared in five rows across two
// tables, `database` in three, `crypto` in two. A reader asking "does describe work?" found it
// under Supported and under Not-supported simultaneously, and could not answer their own
// question. It also led with a five-term glossary, which is the wrong thing to hand someone
// before they know whether they care.
//
// Every status here is still GENERATED from the classifier's own maps — the grouping changed,
// not the source of truth. The audit trail moved to /receipts.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import data from '../data/capabilities.json';

const title = 'What Fidelic can run — capability by capability';
const description =
  'What works, what works with a difference, and what is refused — grouped by the thing you would actually search for, and generated from the classifier itself.';

const LABEL = {
  full: 'Works',
  partial: 'Mostly works',
  none: 'Not supported',
};

// The test-runner block is OPTIONAL on purpose.
//
// capabilities.json is generated by sfemu's scripts/gen-capability-matrix.sh, and a data file
// written before the runner existed has no `tests` key. Reading it unguarded would break the
// build of a site whose copy is otherwise fine — so the section renders only when the figures
// are there, and the page is honest either way: absent means unpublished, never zero.
const tests = (data as any).tests ?? null;
const CORPUS_LABEL = {
  'apex-recipes': 'Salesforce apex-recipes',
  dlrs: 'Declarative Lookup Rollup Summaries',
  eda: 'Education Data Architecture',
  'fflib-apex-common-samplecode': 'fflib sample code',
  npsp: 'Nonprofit Success Pack',
};
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="back"><a href="/">← Home</a></p>
    <h1>What Fidelic can run</h1>

    <p>
      Your org is the real answer, and <a href="#yours">one command gives it to you</a>. This is
      the general shape: what works, and where it differs from a real org.
    </p>

    <h2>The words on this page</h2>
    <dl class="vocab">
      <dt><span class="s s-full">Works</span></dt>
      <dd>Your real Apex runs, and behaves as the org would.</dd>
      <dt><span class="s s-partial">Mostly works</span></dt>
      <dd>Your real Apex runs, but something listed beside it is modelled rather than
      really done, or one corner of it is refused. The row says which.</dd>
      <dt><span class="s s-none">Not supported</span></dt>
      <dd>Refused by name, with a reason and a file and line. Never silently wrong.</dd>
    </dl>

    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table class="caps">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Capability</th><th>Status</th><th>Where it differs from a real org</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.groups.map((g) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                <strong>{g.title}</strong>
                <span class="covers">{g.covers}</span>
              </td>
              <td><span class={`s s-${g.status}`}>{LABEL[g.status]}</span></td>
              <td>{g.summary}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="fine">
      Anything not listed is refused rather than guessed at: you get the file, the line, and the
      construct we do not support. Refusing is the point — a wrong answer you cannot see is
      worse than a gap you can plan around.
    </p>

    {tests && (
      <Fragment>
        <h2 id="tests">Your Apex test classes</h2>
        <p>
          Fidelic runs <strong>simple test classes</strong>. Not your suite — a fraction of it,
          and the fraction is published here rather than described. A test class runs if what it
          does is inside the capabilities above; most real test classes do something that is not.
        </p>
        <p>
          Measured over {tests.testClasses.toLocaleString()} test classes and{' '}
          {tests.testMethods.toLocaleString()} <code>@isTest</code> methods in five open-source
          Salesforce codebases, pinned at a commit:
        </p>

        <ul class="headline">
          <li>
            <strong>{tests.cleanClasses} of {tests.testClasses} test classes</strong>{' '}
            ({tests.cleanClassPercent}%) ran every method end to end with nothing refused.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>{tests.methodsWithAVerdict.toLocaleString()} of{' '}
            {tests.testMethods.toLocaleString()} methods</strong>{' '}
            ({tests.methodVerdictPercent}%) ran and reported a result —{' '}
            {tests.passed.toLocaleString()} passed, {tests.failed.toLocaleString()} failed.
          </li>
          <li>
            Everything else was <strong>refused by name</strong>, with the file, the line and the
            construct. Nothing was skipped quietly and nothing was reported as passing because it
            did not run.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <p class="fine">
          <strong>On those {tests.failed.toLocaleString()} failures.</strong> A failure means the
          assertion compared two values and they differed — sometimes this emulator diverging from
          a real org, sometimes a test that depends on org state we do not model, like profiles or
          record types. We have not split those two and will not guess at the split.
        </p>
        <p class="fine">
          <strong>An ambiguous error counts against us, not against your test.</strong> Only a
          message carrying the runner's own <code>Assertion Failed</code> prefix is scored as a
          failure. Everything else is scored as our refusal even when it surfaces inside your
          test — a query exception because we carry no schema for <code>ApexClass</code>, or a
          wrong-arity call because method overloading is not modelled. Counting those the other
          way would have made this page look better by several points, which is why it does not.
        </p>

        <div class="tablewrap">
          <table class="caps">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Codebase</th>
                <th>Classes that run clean</th>
                <th>Methods with a result</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {tests.corpora.map((c) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <strong>{CORPUS_LABEL[c.corpus] ?? c.corpus}</strong>
                    <span class="covers">{c.testClasses} classes, {c.testMethods.toLocaleString()} methods</span>
                  </td>
                  <td>{c.cleanClasses} of {c.testClasses}</td>
                  <td>{(c.passed + c.failed).toLocaleString()} of {c.testMethods.toLocaleString()}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p class="fine">
          The spread is the useful part. EDA runs {tests.corpora.find((c) => c.corpus === 'eda')?.cleanClasses} of{' '}
          {tests.corpora.find((c) => c.corpus === 'eda')?.testClasses} classes clean; NPSP runs{' '}
          {tests.corpora.find((c) => c.corpus === 'npsp')?.cleanClasses} of{' '}
          {tests.corpora.find((c) => c.corpus === 'npsp')?.testClasses}. Whether your suite is
          nearer one end or the other depends on your code, not on us — which is why the next
          section exists.
        </p>

        <p class="fine">
          One more number, because leaving it out would make this page look better than the
          product is: of the classes our analyzer says it can run, <strong>{tests.gapRatePercent}%
          still refuse once actually run</strong>. We publish the measured result, not the
          prediction, and we are closing that gap in the open.
        </p>

        <p>Point it at your own classes:</p>
        <pre><code>curl -s localhost:8080/__emulator__/testrun            # what would run
curl -sX POST localhost:8080/__emulator__/testrun     # run it</code></pre>
        <p class="fine">
          The GET costs nothing and tells you how much of your suite is even visible before you
          spend a run on it. A run where any method failed returns HTTP 417, so a CI step sees it
          without parsing the body.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
    )}

    <h2 id="yours">The answer for your org</h2>
    <p>
      A general table cannot tell you whether <em>your</em> code runs; that depends on what your
      triggers actually do. Point the analyzer at your metadata and it grades every trigger you
      have, naming the reason for each refusal:
    </p>
    <pre><code>docker run --rm -v "$PWD/mdapi":/mdapi:ro \
  --entrypoint /sfapex fidelic/emulator coverage --report /mdapi</code></pre>
    <p class="fine">
      Nothing leaves your machine — it reads local files and makes no network calls. The report
      is plain text, built for pasting into a channel.
    </p>

    <p class="fine">
      Your coverage report uses more precise words for the same ideas — EXECUTE, SIMULATED,
      UNRESOLVED — and <a href="/receipts/#vocabulary">defines each one</a> alongside the numbers
      behind this page.
    </p>

    <p class="receipts-link">
      <a href="/receipts/">Every number, and the command that produced it →</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .tablewrap{overflow-x:auto}
  table.caps{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:.95rem;margin:1rem 0 1.25rem}
  .caps th,.caps td{text-align:left;padding:.7rem .7rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top}
  .caps th{font-size:.78rem;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.04em;color:var(--muted)}
  .caps td:first-child{min-width:12rem}
  .covers{display:block;color:var(--muted);font-size:.84rem;margin-top:.15rem}
  .s{display:inline-block;font-size:.78rem;font-weight:600;padding:.12rem .5rem;border-radius:999px;white-space:nowrap}
  .s-full{background:rgba(63,185,80,.15);color:#56D364}
  .s-partial{background:rgba(217,163,58,.15);color:#E3B341}
  .s-none{background:rgba(248,81,73,.15);color:#FF8A80}
  .none{color:var(--muted);font-size:.88rem}
  .fine{font-size:.9rem;color:var(--muted)}
  .vocab dt{margin-top:.55rem}
  .vocab dd{margin:.15rem 0 .4rem 0;font-size:.93rem}
  .receipts-link{margin-top:2rem;font-size:.95rem}
  ul.headline{margin:.9rem 0 1.1rem;padding-left:1.15rem}
  ul.headline li{margin:.4rem 0}
  @media (max-width:640px){
    table.caps{font-size:.88rem}
    .caps td:first-child{min-width:8rem}
  }
</style>
